::SPEECH_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which sentence uses the clearest academic language during speech feedback?
{
~A. Your speech had good stuff in it.#This is too vague. Academic language should name the specific communication skill or speech part.
=B. Your evidence supports the main claim clearly.#Correct. This uses precise academic vocabulary and explains what worked in the speech.
~C. The thing you said was nice.#This does not identify the speech element or explain why it was effective.
~D. It sounded okay, I guess.#This is vague and uncertain. It does not help the speaker understand what to keep or improve.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which word is the best academic replacement for "stuff" in a speech discussion?
{
~A. things#This is still vague. It does not name the specific information being discussed.
~B. whatever#This is informal and does not clarify meaning.
~C. something#This is also vague. Academic language should be more precise.
=D. evidence#Correct. Evidence is a precise academic word for information used to support a claim.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which feedback best explains a communication issue?
{
=A. The transition between your first and second reason needs to be clearer.#Correct. This names the issue and points to a specific place in the speech.
~B. The middle was weird.#This is vague and does not explain what should improve.
~C. It was not good there.#This does not identify the problem or the location clearly.
~D. You should talk better.#This is too general to help the speaker revise.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.08
Question:
Which response uses academic language while still supporting collaboration?
{
~A. Your part is bad, and mine is better.#This attacks the classmate and does not support collaboration.
~B. I do not like it, so change everything.#This gives an opinion but not academic feedback.
=C. Your claim is clear; we could add evidence so the audience understands it better.#Correct. This uses academic language, gives respectful feedback, and suggests a collaborative improvement.
~D. I will fix it myself because you cannot.#This is disrespectful and removes collaboration.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L04_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L04_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.10
Question:
Which statement best explains the purpose of academic language in oral communication?
{
~A. It makes the speaker sound more complicated.#This is a misconception. Academic language should clarify meaning, not make speech harder to understand.
=B. It helps the audience understand precise ideas clearly.#Correct. Academic language uses exact words so listeners can follow the speaker's meaning.
~C. It replaces the need for examples.#This is incorrect. Academic language and examples can work together to support understanding.
~D. It allows speakers to avoid explaining their ideas.#This is the opposite of the lesson concept. Academic language should support explanation.
}
